  <h2 id="what-is-resolution">What is Video Resolution?</h2>
  <p>Video resolution refers to the number of distinct pixels in each dimension that can be displayed. It is usually quoted as width × height, with the units in pixels (e.g., 1920×1080). A higher resolution means more pixels, which translates to a sharper and more detailed image.</p>

  <h2 id="standard-resolutions">Standard Resolutions (360p to 4K)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>360p (Standard Definition):</strong> 640×360 pixels. Suitable for very slow internet connections or old mobile devices.</li>
    <li><strong>480p (DVD Quality):</strong> 854×480 pixels. The standard for DVDs. Good enough for casual viewing on small screens.</li>
    <li><strong>720p (HD):</strong> 1280×720 pixels. The entry-level high definition. Great for web streaming and most mobile devices.</li>
    <li><strong>1080p (Full HD):</strong> 1920×1080 pixels. The standard for modern web video, Blu-rays, and standard TVs. Offers a great balance of quality and file size.</li>
    <li><strong>1440p (Quad HD / 2K):</strong> 2560×1440 pixels. Popular among PC gamers and higher-end smartphones.</li>
    <li><strong>2160p (4K UHD):</strong> 3840×2160 pixels. Offers four times the pixels of 1080p. Best for large 4K TVs and future-proofing your downloads.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="which-to-choose">Which Resolution to Choose When Downloading?</h2>
  <p>When using a video downloader, choose <strong>1080p</strong> for the best balance of visual fidelity and manageable file size. Only choose <strong>4K</strong> if you plan to watch the video on a large 4K display and have plenty of storage space.</p>
